# cur-kit

This project runs Spec Kit through cur-kit.

- Always Run: when "test" is set in `.cursor/cur-kit.json`, the suite runs after every completed turn. A red result comes back as the next message.
- During /speckit-implement, the parent does not write application code or tests. It gives every implementation task to `cur-kit-writer` and waits.
- The parent plans. Children write code through the `cur-kit-writer` agent, one task each, on separate files. Run up to twelve writers at once, and more when the job is large. Scouts run up to twelve at once, and more when the research is large.
- Commits and pushes are the parent's. Children never commit or push.
- Pick a child by its agent type and do not pass a model. Each agent file in `.cursor/agents/` sets its own.
- Test names cite the keys they prove: `FR-###`, `SC-###`, `USn`, or `USn/ACn`, for example `test_US1_AC2_redirects`.
- Nothing is marked [x] while the suite is red, or before `cur-kit-reviewer` has matched the scenario to the test.
- The stop hook may submit `/speckit-converge` after `/speckit-implement`, and `/speckit-implement` when converge asks for it. Otherwise the human types a skill, or runs `specify workflow run .cursor/cur-kit/workflow.yml`.
- One writer at a time edits `spec.md`, `plan.md`, `research.md`, `tasks.md`, checklists, `CHANGELOG.md`, and `.cursor/cur-kit.json`. Children send changes to these files back to the parent.
- Secrets never go in messages, prompts, or agent files. Refer to a secret by its environment variable name, like `$STRIPE_KEY`.
- After each child returns, record it as one JSON line in `.cursor/cur-kit/state/slots.jsonl`, for example `{"agent": "cur-kit-writer", "task": "T014", "files": ["src/auth.py", "tests/test_auth.py"], "result": "green"}`.
